.TH MACHO\-DISASM 1 "October 4, 2026" "MachO-explorer 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
macho\-disasm \- decode instruction lengths and direct branch targets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B macho\-disasm
.RI [ hex-addr ]
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RI [ count ]
.RB [ \-\-branches ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-\-arch= name ]
.RB [ \-b | \-\-binary
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Decodes instructions from an address to the end of the code section, reporting
where each one starts, how long it is, and where it branches to. With
.B \-\-branches
it reports only the branches, as resolved
.RI { from , to }
edges.
.PP
This is what
.BR macho\-findcall (1)
cannot do on its own.
.B macho\-findcall
finds the five bytes
.B e8 xx xx xx xx
and reports them as call sites, but the same five bytes occur inside longer
instructions and inside data, so it has no way to know that a given one *begins*
an instruction. Its output is a shortlist of places worth looking at, and it says
so. This tool knows where instructions start and end, which is what turns that
shortlist into edges.
.PP
With no address, the decode starts at the beginning of the first code section.
That start is trustworthy only in the sense that it is a section boundary, so
prefer a symbol address when one is available:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-symlookup 0x100085c30 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go
macho\-disasm 0x100085c30 /usr/local/go/bin/go
.fi
.RE
.SH WHAT THIS IS NOT
No mnemonics, no operand decoding, no control flow graph, no decompilation.
Instructions are reported as bytes plus, where applicable, a branch target and a
branch kind. The boundary is between knowing where instruction boundaries fall
and knowing what the program does, and this stays on the near side of it.
.PP
Only
.B direct\-relative
branches are resolved.
.I x86_64
reports
.BR CALL ,
.BR JMP ,
.BR Jcc ,
.B LOOP
and
.BR JRCXZ .
RIP\-relative operands such as
.B call qword ptr [rip+disp32]
are deliberately
.I not
reported as branches: the displacement addresses a pointer, not code, and
treating it as a branch displacement fabricates a target roughly 2<sup>32</sup>
bytes away. Locating those is
.BR macho\-mapliteral (1)'s
job.
.PP
.BR arm64 / arm64e
report
.BR BL ,
.BR B ,
.BR B.cond ,
.BR CBZ ,
.BR CBNZ ,
.BR TBZ ,
.BR TBNZ ,
.B ADRP
and
.BR ADR .
Note that
.B ADRP
is page\-relative and
.B ADR
is not scaled, so neither is "pc plus a displacement" in the way
.B BL
is.
.SH SWEEPING IS NOT DISCOVERY
The decode is a
.I linear sweep
in address order, not a recursive descent. It decodes every byte of the range, so
alignment padding and any data interleaved in the code section are decoded as
instructions too. The summary line reports how many bytes were covered and how
many instructions that produced, so a region with a poor instruction\-per\-byte
ratio is visible rather than something to be guessed at.
.PP
The warning applies with much more force to
.I x86_64
than to
.IR arm64 .
ARM64 instructions are all four bytes, so boundaries cannot drift. An
.I x86_64
length\-decoding mistake shifts every boundary after it, and
.B src/instruction.mjs
documents the known gaps in its opcode table:
.BR 3DNow! ,
AMD
.BR extrq / insertq ,
EVEX opcodes that take an immediate, and APX. All of those are
.I short
readings, so the damage is a sweep that desynchronises at that instruction, never
a fabricated address.
.PP
Sweeping a whole
.B __text
from its start was measured against
.BR /usr/lib/dyld :
91.0% of the section's symbols landed on an instruction boundary when the sweep
began at the section start, against 100% when each sweep began at its own
symbol's address. That gap is the difference between a coverage report and a
decoding.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-branches
Report only branches, as resolved edges. The byte column is dropped, because an
edge is the thing asked for.
.TP
.BI \-\-count= n
Stop after
.I n
instructions. Default 32.
.B 0
means no cap, which is only meaningful together with
.BR \-\-bytes ;
decoding a whole code section of a large binary is millions of records, and
offering that by accident would be a footgun wearing a useful feature's clothes.
Must be a non\-negative integer.
.TP
.BI \-\-bytes= n
Decode
.I n
bytes rather than a count of instructions. With
.B \-\-bytes
and no
.BR \-\-count ,
a range can be given exactly.
.TP
.BI \-\-arch= name
Read one architecture by name:
.BR arm64 ,
.BR arm64e ,
or
.BR x86_64 .
As elsewhere in this package the value is a preference, not a requirement: if
that slice is absent another is read and a note says which.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-binary " " \fIpath\fR
The binary to read.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write one JSON object to standard output and send all prose to standard error.
Addresses are rendered as
.B "0x..."
strings: a 64\-bit virtual address does not survive a JSON number.
.TP
.B \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit 0.
.SH ARCHITECTURES
.BR arm64 ,
.BR arm64e " and " x86_64
are decoded. Any other architecture is reported as
.B unknown\-encoding
and exits 3 rather than returning an empty result, because "this binary's
instructions are not decoded here" and "there is nothing at that address" are
different answers and a caller needs to be able to tell them apart.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MACHO_BINARY
The binary to read when none is given.
.TP
.B MACHO_APP
An application bundle to read, tried after
.BR MACHO_BINARY .
.TP
.B MACHO_CONFIG
Path to a replacement
.IR config.json .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one instruction was decoded.
.TP
.B 1
The tool ran and nothing was decoded at the address \- for instance it lies in a
data section, or in no section at all. A negative result, not a failure.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, including an unknown flag or a non\-numeric count.
.TP
.B 3
The file could not be read or parsed, or the slice has no instruction decoder.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Where do instructions start at this address, and where do they go?
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-disasm 0x100085c30 /usr/local/go/bin/go
.fi
.RE
.PP
Every direct branch in the next 4 KiB, as edges:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-disasm \-\-branches \-\-bytes=4096 \-\-count=0 \-b /usr/local/go/bin/go
.fi
.RE
.PP
What
.B macho\-findcall
found, resolved to real instruction boundaries:
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-disasm \-\-json 0x100085c30 64 /usr/local/go/bin/go \
  | jq -r '.data.instructions[] | select(.kind) | "\(.addr) \(.kind) \(.target)"'
.fi
.RE
.PP
.A "On Apple Silicon, read the arm64 slice explicitly:"
.PP
.RS
.nf
macho\-disasm \-\-arch=arm64 \-\-count=16 /usr/local/go/bin/go
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
Positional order is
.RI [ hex-addr ]
.RI [ binary | bundle ]
.RI [ count ].
The address is recognised by its
.B 0x
prefix and is optional; the count is a bare integer, and anything else in that
slot is the binary. A count is never a path, so the two cannot be confused.
.PP
A decoded instruction that runs past the end of the requested range is reported
with its full length and clipped, rather than silently dropped \- so the record
count still adds up to what was asked for.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR macho\-findcall (1),
.BR macho\-mapliteral (1),
.BR macho\-symlookup (1),
.BR macho\-describe (1),
.BR otool (1),
.BR objdump (1),
.BR nm (1)